{"team":"PHI","col":"#3FB7B0","identity":"run-heavy","funnel":"balanced-D","plays_pg":61.7,"adot":8.3,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>Built to Run</h2><p>The backfield runs through one man \u2014 <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong> absorbs <strong>77% of the carries</strong> (league ~59%), a true bell-cow share that makes his rushing volume among the safest floors in the sport when the script cooperates.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>The WR2 is unusually involved here \u2014 <strong>23%</strong> of targets (league ~15%), meaning there's bankable volume beyond the top receiver.</p><p>Run identity is the through-line here: PHI call pass on <strong>-6%</strong> of snaps relative to what the situation expects (league -2%), and when they get ahead they lean even harder on the ground \u2014 just 37% pass on early downs with a lead. This is a ball-control operation built to shorten games.</p><p>They lean on tempo \u2014 a 23% no-huddle rate (league ~11%) that inflates snap counts and everyone's volume.</p><p>They live in the shotgun (81% of snaps vs ~68%) \u2014 a spread, pass-oriented structure.</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Inside the 20 they lean run-heavy, passing <strong>36%</strong> of the time, funnelling touchdown equity to <strong>Saquon Barkley</strong>.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 A Balanced Defense</h2><p>They lock down opposing tight ends \u2014 just 25 rec yds/game (vs ~35); fade receiving props there.</p><p><strong>Soft against opposing WR2s</strong> \u2014 42 rec yds/game (vs ~36). A rec-yards target.</p><p>They lock down opposing WR1s \u2014 just 52 rec yds/game (vs ~58); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They surrender <strong>112 rushing</strong> and <strong>208 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). No dominant funnel \u2014 read it matchup by matchup.</p><p>A stingy, efficient defense \u2014 just 41% success allowed (vs ~44%); temper expectations for opposing skill players.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: back <stron